WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.363
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.363 --> 00:00:09.982
&gt;&gt; We have a chance tonight to talk about,
an extraordinary

00:00:09.982 --> 00:00:14.970
book, The New Digital Age, which you two
of you collaborated in.

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:16.760
I'm gonna start with the obvious question.

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:20.004
You've said that this is a book, about
technology,

00:00:20.004 --> 00:00:23.608
but more importantly it's a book about
human beings.

00:00:23.608 --> 00:00:27.207
What prompted you to write this book, and
what

00:00:27.207 --> 00:00:31.282
prompted the two of you to write this book
together?

00:00:31.282 --> 00:00:37.110
&gt;&gt; Well, so Eric and I met in Baghdad in,
2000 2009.

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:41.130
He he wanted to see what it was like
travelling

00:00:41.130 --> 00:00:44.610
into a war zone and whether technology was
relevant at all.

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:47.280
And so we show up there and our security

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:51.090
is waiting for us, but Eric's not moving,
because

00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:54.050
he's wearing a flak jacket and, before he
does

00:00:54.050 --> 00:00:56.985
anything, he wants a full history of flak
jackets.

00:00:56.985 --> 00:00:59.609
[LAUGH] How they've adapted, why they
work, how

00:00:59.609 --> 00:01:03.000
they've sort of changed since the
beginning of time.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.780
But after the trip to Iraq we traveled to
more than 40 countries.

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:12.050
Looking at the ways in which technology is
disrupting autocracies.

00:01:12.050 --> 00:01:15.010
Ways that it's changing the nature of
violence on the ground.

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:17.619
And what we realized is, there's an
inherent connection between

00:01:17.619 --> 00:01:20.800
the Silicon Valley world and the
geo-political world that's really missing.

00:01:23.618 --> 00:01:27.580
&gt;&gt; First I, thank you very much Dr. Rice,
Condi for, for doing this with us.

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:29.020
It's a great privilege to be here.

00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:33.180
I do have one request which is I want
everyone to turn on their phones.

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:35.500
We're in the phone business.

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:36.830
We want you using your phone.

00:01:36.830 --> 00:01:38.935
We make money when you're doing that.

00:01:38.935 --> 00:01:44.095
[LAUGH] The what happened with Iraq was
that my daughter Sophie who's a student

00:01:44.095 --> 00:01:47.597
here at GSP and I've showed up in Iraq and
of course we, we put

00:01:47.597 --> 00:01:51.612
the flack jackets on and we meet this guy
Jared, and she was videotaping the

00:01:51.612 --> 00:01:56.980
trip for various reasons and we played
them back all we heard was Jared's voice.

00:01:56.980 --> 00:02:00.610
To fast forward, Sophie ultimately took
the book that Jared and I

00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:04.901
wrote and she's heavily credited with
writing it into it's current style.

00:02:04.901 --> 00:02:07.731
So thank you Sophie for doing that.

00:02:07.731 --> 00:02:12.490
I'd like you to think about a Secretary of
State.

00:02:12.490 --> 00:02:18.027
Imagine if you'd had a technology arm that
could implement technology

00:02:18.027 --> 00:02:22.652
that would actually fix a problem that was
bedeviling you.

00:02:22.652 --> 00:02:27.649
Censorship, communications, empowering
citizens, empowering women.

00:02:27.649 --> 00:02:30.063
Something that was on your mind, and yet

00:02:30.063 --> 00:02:32.762
all you had were the tools of foreign
policy.

00:02:32.762 --> 00:02:34.720
It just seems like in our industry we

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:38.730
should define ourselves with a somewhat
higher purpose.

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:43.520
Why don't our industry, the tech industry,
figure out a way to solve these problems.

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:47.156
I knew very little about foreign policy
until I met Jerald, right?

00:02:47.156 --> 00:02:49.756
And thank you for hiring him, I'm sure you
sort

00:02:49.756 --> 00:02:52.683
of beat him into submission at some basic
level here.

00:02:52.683 --> 00:02:56.730
So now he can sort of talk to me and say,
look, these are the problems.

00:02:56.730 --> 00:02:58.780
I was struck by the horrific situation
that

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:02.130
most people are in the world, which is

00:03:02.130 --> 00:03:03.910
of governance, the horrific way in which
women

00:03:03.910 --> 00:03:06.960
are treated, the corruption at every level
of government.

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:12.353
The majority of people, humans like us,
don't have any of the benefits of any

00:03:12.353 --> 00:03:14.804
person here in the room, and yet our

00:03:14.804 --> 00:03:18.340
industry spends all most time talking
about that.

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:20.892
So the real genesis of the book was, why
did we not

00:03:20.892 --> 00:03:24.894
only identify what's gonna happen to them,
but also what the bridges are?

00:03:24.894 --> 00:03:28.001
After two years of going through this, we
ultimately come out

00:03:28.001 --> 00:03:31.940
in the book, I would claim, with a pretty
optimistic message.

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:35.152
The world for the well to do world, us, is
going to be

00:03:35.152 --> 00:03:38.292
fantastic in terms of fiber optics,
computations

00:03:38.292 --> 00:03:40.900
and so forth, we'll talk about that.

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:43.410
But for the developing world where you
have no connectivity at

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:46.410
all, the arrival of the smartphone is a
life changing event.

00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:49.803
Because from this single device you solve

00:03:49.803 --> 00:03:54.230
illiteracy, you solve empowerment, you get
better governance.

00:03:54.230 --> 00:03:58.140
And these are folks who don't even have
electric power and running water.

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:00.040
You solve their business problem, their
health

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:02.500
problem, all of their needs can be met.

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:06.620
And over the next five years, another
three or four billion people will join us.

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:11.730
The rough number now is roughly 2.4
billion people on the internet.

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:14.790
Roughly 3.7 people using, humans using,
using phones.

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:15.290
Those numbers

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:19.930
will go to 5 to 6 billion in the next five
years.

00:04:19.930 --> 00:04:22.624
This transition, transformation is life
changing for half

00:04:22.624 --> 00:04:24.909
of the world's population, right in front
of us.

00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:28.157
&gt;&gt; And Derek, let me ask you because

00:04:28.157 --> 00:04:31.170
Eric Wrightly said you've spent time in
the state

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:34.038
department and so you know not just
foreign

00:04:34.038 --> 00:04:38.870
policy, but the business of carrying out
foreign policy.

00:04:38.870 --> 00:04:42.290
What Eric has described, is that going to
happen in spite of governments?

00:04:42.290 --> 00:04:47.390
In spite of our government?

00:04:47.390 --> 00:04:49.810
Or is there a chance that governments
could

00:04:49.810 --> 00:04:53.880
actually help to promote this more
optimistic world?

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:57.470
And what are the impediments to, to what's
being done?

00:04:57.470 --> 00:04:59.780
&gt;&gt; Well I think it, it depends on which
type of government.

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:01.570
So, of the the 5 billion people who are
coming

00:05:01.570 --> 00:05:04.180
online, most of them live in countries
that are autocratic.

00:05:04.180 --> 00:05:07.973
And so there's a race between, you know,
countries like the United States

00:05:07.973 --> 00:05:12.004
and those in Western Europe who wanna see
a global connected citizen re-based on

00:05:12.004 --> 00:05:14.255
principles of the free flow of
information,

00:05:14.255 --> 00:05:16.093
and countries like China and Iran and

00:05:16.093 --> 00:05:18.228
North-Korea and others that wanna see the

00:05:18.228 --> 00:05:21.972
world's technological infrastructure build
with trap doors.

00:05:21.972 --> 00:05:25.741
They wanna see decadence, you know, have
at least surveilled and censored.

00:05:25.741 --> 00:05:28.939
And there really is an open question of,
of who is gonna win.

00:05:28.939 --> 00:05:29.027
Right?

00:05:29.027 --> 00:05:32.107
So, you know, technology, it's inevitable
that all these

00:05:32.107 --> 00:05:34.892
people will be connected, but there is a
very important

00:05:34.892 --> 00:05:37.441
need for a human intervention to ensure
that the con

00:05:37.441 --> 00:05:42.022
activity that they're getting, lends
itself towards more democratic principles.

00:05:42.022 --> 00:05:47.565
&gt;&gt; Let me throw out a few country names
and tell me how you think technology will

00:05:47.565 --> 00:05:53.466
impact those countries and the problems
that they present for foreign policy.

00:05:53.466 --> 00:05:56.022
And I'm going to take foreign policy, in

00:05:56.022 --> 00:05:59.236
this sense, not just American foreign
policy, but

00:05:59.236 --> 00:06:02.377
let's just say countries that are more
interested

00:06:02.377 --> 00:06:05.610
in an open, more liberal, more democratic
world.

00:06:05.610 --> 00:06:08.960
So, not just the United States but the

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:11.350
countries that would be in that that
category.

00:06:11.350 --> 00:06:15.540
You went to one of the, maybe the hardest
case that I can think of, North Korea.

00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:21.830
What in the world were you doing in North
Korea?

00:06:21.830 --> 00:06:23.245
Did you see Dennis Rodman?

00:06:23.245 --> 00:06:23.460
[LAUGH].

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:24.080
&gt;&gt; He, he, he came afterwards.

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:24.220
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:06:24.220 --> 00:06:24.750
What, what was it like?

00:06:24.750 --> 00:06:26.140
&gt;&gt; Yeah, but, but, but let's, let's just
establish cause.

00:06:26.140 --> 00:06:26.280
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:26.740
&gt;&gt; Causality here.

00:06:26.740 --> 00:06:27.010
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:06:27.010 --> 00:06:27.250
Yeah, yeah.

00:06:27.250 --> 00:06:28.249
All right.

00:06:28.249 --> 00:06:29.618
&gt;&gt; This was Jared's idea.

00:06:29.618 --> 00:06:33.695
[LAUGH] And I discovered, you know?

00:06:33.695 --> 00:06:34.806
I have spent a lot of time with Jared.

00:06:34.806 --> 00:06:37.507
I've discovered he's never been to South
Korea.

00:06:37.507 --> 00:06:40.860
Can you imagine going to North Korea and
never having been to South Korea?

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:43.488
So once again Jared and I my daughter
Sophie we

00:06:43.488 --> 00:06:46.542
decide in our little merry band to go to
North Korea.

00:06:46.542 --> 00:06:51.754
And my impression was, there's gotta be
something that we can do.

00:06:51.754 --> 00:06:54.800
They have millions a million cell phones
22 million people.

00:06:55.880 --> 00:06:58.792
That's a very poor country with horrific,
horrific

00:06:58.792 --> 00:07:01.705
governance obviously and, maybe if we
could just get

00:07:01.705 --> 00:07:03.452
to turn on a little bit about that

00:07:03.452 --> 00:07:06.250
internet, we could begin to open up the
country.

00:07:06.250 --> 00:07:08.970
The interesting thing is, and we met and

00:07:08.970 --> 00:07:13.130
they treated us well especially given it's
a repressive

00:07:13.130 --> 00:07:16.810
state but, most importantly they let us
out and,

00:07:16.810 --> 00:07:20.650
it's a country where there's only one
decision maker.

00:07:23.510 --> 00:07:25.933
And the only way that country will open up

00:07:25.933 --> 00:07:29.138
without a revolution, which is difficult,
is id the

00:07:29.138 --> 00:07:32.844
leader decides that the country needs the
information, more

00:07:32.844 --> 00:07:36.211
than it needs the conflict, that new ideas
will bring.

00:07:36.211 --> 00:07:45.490
I think, what we concluded was that all
you have to do is to insert doubt.

00:07:45.490 --> 00:07:49.498
North Korea is a true autocratic state,
true essentially.

00:07:49.498 --> 00:07:50.505
It's the last one.

00:07:50.505 --> 00:07:53.264
They really do believe from birth that
their

00:07:53.264 --> 00:07:56.330
leader is god, king, religion, and so
forth.

00:07:56.330 --> 00:08:01.620
All we have to do is get a little bit of
doubt in, and that country will fall over.

00:08:01.620 --> 00:08:02.410
&gt;&gt; Also to, to, to add to this-.

00:08:02.410 --> 00:08:05.250
&gt;&gt; Actually you need to, need to tell them
about

00:08:05.250 --> 00:08:09.008
the, the weird stuff we saw like the, the,
the trophies.

00:08:09.008 --> 00:08:09.058
[CROSSTALK].

00:08:09.058 --> 00:08:10.860
&gt;&gt; So we, so this is, this is a-.

00:08:10.860 --> 00:08:12.213
&gt;&gt; Typical of Jared, so we go in right?

00:08:12.213 --> 00:08:15.760
[LAUGH] Hang on, hang on all right I have
to set it up then you can describe it.

00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:17.551
So we go into this mausoleum, right?

00:08:17.551 --> 00:08:19.729
Where they've got this guy embalmed,
right?

00:08:19.729 --> 00:08:21.915
It's like a really big deal and they take
us

00:08:21.915 --> 00:08:24.540
to this trophy case and he's got all these
awards.

00:08:24.540 --> 00:08:25.649
&gt;&gt; Right but from your laundry list

00:08:25.649 --> 00:08:27.740
of really problematic countries like
Equatorial Guinea.

00:08:27.740 --> 00:08:27.953
&gt;&gt; I didn't know that-.

00:08:27.953 --> 00:08:28.003
&gt;&gt; Central Africa Republic.

00:08:28.003 --> 00:08:31.282
&gt;&gt; I didn't know that they were really bad
countries.

00:08:31.282 --> 00:08:34.375
&gt;&gt; So you go to there and they have this
big trophy case that they want to

00:08:34.375 --> 00:08:36.746
draw your attention to, and it's they have
a

00:08:36.746 --> 00:08:40.337
honorary doctorate from an American
university called Kensington University.

00:08:40.337 --> 00:08:42.641
They didn't let us have any connectivity
when we were there so

00:08:42.641 --> 00:08:45.586
we didn't wanna be intellectual snobby and
say we've never heard of it.

00:08:45.586 --> 00:08:46.978
So we sort of take their word for it.

00:08:46.978 --> 00:08:48.249
&gt;&gt; What were the other countries?

00:08:48.249 --> 00:08:52.700
&gt;&gt; It was, it was Pakistan, Venezuela,
Cuba, Iran.

00:08:52.700 --> 00:08:53.979
Like some real winners.

00:08:53.979 --> 00:08:54.150
[LAUGH].

00:08:54.150 --> 00:08:54.440
&gt;&gt; And so-.

00:08:54.440 --> 00:08:56.410
&gt;&gt; And medals, plaques, gold-.

00:08:56.410 --> 00:08:56.920
&gt;&gt; But this

00:08:58.980 --> 00:09:01.110
American, this, this Kensington
University, we

00:09:01.110 --> 00:09:02.430
sort of took their word for it.

00:09:02.430 --> 00:09:04.680
And then we left North Korea and went to
China, which

00:09:04.680 --> 00:09:08.230
we all know is a bastion of democracy
relative to North Korea.

00:09:08.230 --> 00:09:09.880
And what's the first thing that we do?

00:09:09.880 --> 00:09:13.076
We do a little Google search of Kensington
University,

00:09:13.076 --> 00:09:15.728
which was founded by a law partner at a
boutique

00:09:15.728 --> 00:09:18.448
law firm in Texas, who figured out that he
could

00:09:18.448 --> 00:09:22.940
launder money by selling honorary
doctorates to third world dictators.

00:09:22.940 --> 00:09:26.438
[LAUGH] Now somehow this guy graduated
from law school passed the

00:09:26.438 --> 00:09:30.200
bar, but wasn't the brightest person in
the world because when the

00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:33.236
FBI found out about this, he fled Texas
thinking he was

00:09:33.236 --> 00:09:36.635
leaving the country only to be arrested by
the FBI in Hawaii.

00:09:36.635 --> 00:09:39.160
[LAUGH] But back to your serious question
about.

00:09:39.160 --> 00:09:41.190
&gt;&gt; No, no, no just finish the story.

00:09:41.190 --> 00:09:44.360
So we then said, what do we do with this
information?

00:09:44.360 --> 00:09:48.180
And we realized that if we, had told them,
they would of, ignore it.

00:09:50.180 --> 00:09:52.450
That was the most bizarre thing about
North Korea.

00:09:52.450 --> 00:09:55.984
&gt;&gt; But, there was also, there, there's an
even more serious part of

00:09:55.984 --> 00:09:59.270
this, which is before we went to North
Korea we met with a number

00:09:59.270 --> 00:10:03.486
of defectors and the defectors told us
that the penalty for being caught with

00:10:03.486 --> 00:10:07.850
a smuggled smartphone in North Korea, can
in some cases be the death penalty.

00:10:07.850 --> 00:10:11.690
And then they persecute three generations
of your family, and people take that risk

00:10:11.690 --> 00:10:16.350
and then take it again to get a signal
from across the Chinese border.

00:10:16.350 --> 00:10:19.170
So people like Eric and me, and really all
of us in this room, we have no frame

00:10:19.170 --> 00:10:22.370
of reference for wanting access and
connectivity so badly

00:10:22.370 --> 00:10:24.840
that we're willing to risk being shot for
it.

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:27.480
And so we wanted to go to the country that
is so

00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:31.470
extreme that these are the consequences,
and understand what it looks like.

00:10:31.470 --> 00:10:34.880
And the conclusion, as Eric mentioned, is
if you think back to the

00:10:34.880 --> 00:10:36.970
days of the Cold War, there were

00:10:36.970 --> 00:10:39.470
totalitarian societies, cults of
personality, et cetera.

00:10:39.470 --> 00:10:42.210
In the future you're still gonna have
autocracies.

00:10:42.210 --> 00:10:44.810
You're still gonna have horrible
societies.

00:10:44.810 --> 00:10:46.990
But the one silver lining in of all this

00:10:46.990 --> 00:10:51.610
is, the totalitarian societies, the true
cults of personality have

00:10:51.610 --> 00:10:53.880
literally been eliminated by the internet
in the same way

00:10:53.880 --> 00:10:58.010
that scientists were able to get rid of
small pox.

00:10:58.010 --> 00:11:01.700
Once North Korea changes, you'll never see
a culted personality again because

00:11:01.700 --> 00:11:04.835
the ability to create a society without
doubt will no longer be possible.

00:11:04.835 --> 00:11:06.030
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:11:06.030 --> 00:11:12.440
So, there are societies in which you wish
to instill doubt, like North Korea.

00:11:12.440 --> 00:11:15.998
By the way, my favorite story about North
Korea is that some of

00:11:15.998 --> 00:11:19.186
you may know that I've taken up golf, and
I really love it.

00:11:19.186 --> 00:11:21.594
Kim Jong Un will shot 39.

00:11:21.594 --> 00:11:22.114
&gt;&gt; Right, that's very impressive.

00:11:22.114 --> 00:11:23.288
&gt;&gt; Period.

00:11:23.288 --> 00:11:25.629
&gt;&gt; Not on the front 39.

00:11:25.629 --> 00:11:28.952
And apparently it was so easy, the game,
that he gave it up.

00:11:28.952 --> 00:11:32.597
So that tells you something about about
this regime.

00:11:32.597 --> 00:11:37.296
But, so you've got the regimes like North
Korea.

00:11:37.296 --> 00:11:40.662
You also have regimes that are more
integrated into the

00:11:40.662 --> 00:11:45.134
international system, but are doing
terrible things to their people.

00:11:45.134 --> 00:11:49.939
Is there anything that you see,
technologically, technology playing a role

00:11:49.939 --> 00:11:55.199
in alleviating, for instance, let's take
the suffering in Syria, at this point.

00:11:55.199 --> 00:11:59.684
Where people, you talk about connectivity,
but one problem is if you

00:11:59.684 --> 00:12:04.640
are a Syrian refugee, you have left
everything behind that mattered to you.

00:12:04.640 --> 00:12:10.171
Is there anything that technology can do
for the Syrian refugee for instance?

00:12:10.171 --> 00:12:13.055
&gt;&gt; I was, I was just on the, the Syrian
border about three

00:12:13.055 --> 00:12:16.125
weeks ago and I went to a number of
refugee camps in, in

00:12:16.125 --> 00:12:18.887
northeastern, northeastern Lebanon and
turns out

00:12:18.887 --> 00:12:20.830
the big, one of the biggest problems

00:12:20.830 --> 00:12:22.587
that the refugees have among many

00:12:22.587 --> 00:12:25.630
is dealing with the refugee organization
bureaucracy.

00:12:25.630 --> 00:12:27.975
It's so bad and so difficult to get papers
that

00:12:27.975 --> 00:12:31.358
refugees are literally going back to Syria
to get documentation.

00:12:31.358 --> 00:12:33.575
So you think about, you think about the
risk.

00:12:33.575 --> 00:12:36.572
But the, the, the problem that you have in
Syria is

00:12:36.572 --> 00:12:39.990
the Syria context, you know basically
creates a bug in our

00:12:39.990 --> 00:12:44.739
technological optimism because you have
literally thousands of videos that are

00:12:44.739 --> 00:12:49.190
coming out of Syria, each one more
horrific than, than the next.

00:12:49.190 --> 00:12:51.900
And it's doing nothing to increase
political

00:12:51.900 --> 00:12:53.760
will on the part of states to intervene.

00:12:53.760 --> 00:12:56.250
At the end of the day, without a state led
intervention

00:12:56.250 --> 00:12:59.560
of some kind, you know, the horror is not
gonna stop.

00:12:59.560 --> 00:13:02.720
I heard some horrible stories from Syrian
friends of mine that I hadn't

00:13:02.720 --> 00:13:06.390
seen since I was living there in 2004 and
2005 about government checkpoints in

00:13:06.390 --> 00:13:10.150
Damascus, Holmes and Aleppo, where they
stop you and ask for your phone,

00:13:10.150 --> 00:13:13.600
they hold a gun to your head and ask for
your log in information.

00:13:13.600 --> 00:13:19.870
They then look at what's been posted on
your wall by you or somebody else.

00:13:19.870 --> 00:13:22.360
My friend's brother told me that when they
did

00:13:22.360 --> 00:13:24.510
my friend told me that when their brother
did

00:13:24.510 --> 00:13:27.550
this they saw someone had posted a page
sympathetic

00:13:27.550 --> 00:13:30.770
to the revolution on one of his social
networking platforms.

00:13:30.770 --> 00:13:32.850
And a signal came from the checkpoint to
the top

00:13:32.850 --> 00:13:34.409
of a building where they then shot him in
the head.

00:13:35.450 --> 00:13:38.100
You know, technology can't fix that
problem.

00:13:38.100 --> 00:13:41.830
This is a brutal regime doing terrible
things and I think it's important that

00:13:41.830 --> 00:13:44.450
we understand, cuz we're far away from it
out here, that there are limits.

00:13:44.450 --> 00:13:48.420
It's a part of the solution, but at the
end of the day, states are still the

00:13:48.420 --> 00:13:51.269
dominant unit in the international system,
and they're the

00:13:51.269 --> 00:13:54.350
ones that have to take charge of the
situation.

00:13:54.350 --> 00:13:56.810
&gt;&gt; It's [COUGH] it's, so we've been
debating, we've been debating this for a

00:13:56.810 --> 00:14:00.240
while because, you just hate it when you
find a bug in your model, right?

00:14:00.240 --> 00:14:04.300
And we all start from the premises, that
you empower individuals and the thing

00:14:04.300 --> 00:14:08.561
that's new here, is this, is the empower
of citizens around the world, right?

00:14:08.561 --> 00:14:12.073
We've never had a situation where people
were so empowered, there's

00:14:12.073 --> 00:14:15.070
also some good and mostly good, but some
bad things as well.

00:14:15.070 --> 00:14:16.948
So where does it breakdown?

00:14:16.948 --> 00:14:18.598
Well the first place in these countries
they

00:14:18.598 --> 00:14:20.839
shut down the internet, because they're in
a war.

00:14:20.839 --> 00:14:23.630
And so that's always a bad, bad deal.

00:14:23.630 --> 00:14:28.330
But ultimately, knowledge and awareness is
not

00:14:28.330 --> 00:14:31.920
the same thing as shooting guns, right.

00:14:31.920 --> 00:14:34.750
So, if you're going to know everything,
which is a

00:14:34.750 --> 00:14:38.560
reasonable presumption of the future,
we're gonna know every massacre.

00:14:38.560 --> 00:14:40.700
We're gonna know the horrors.

00:14:40.700 --> 00:14:46.229
You're gonna have to still have to have
some way of using, of stopping it.

00:14:46.229 --> 00:14:47.492
Right?

00:14:47.492 --> 00:14:48.853
Now you can do it with an international
car.

00:14:48.853 --> 00:14:53.101
In the book we write that you could in
fact begin the criminal trial of

00:14:53.101 --> 00:14:55.857
the criminal, the war criminals during the

00:14:55.857 --> 00:14:59.155
time they're doing the war, the, the
criminals.

00:14:59.155 --> 00:14:59.752
Right?

00:14:59.752 --> 00:15:02.513
And there's evidence that this kind of
awareness

00:15:02.513 --> 00:15:05.146
is reducing the number of people being
killed.

00:15:05.146 --> 00:15:08.954
So a cynical of talking about the U,
Ukraine is to say that

00:15:08.954 --> 00:15:13.240
only 78 people were killed in the square,
and they got a new government.

00:15:13.240 --> 00:15:17.060
And obviously every death is bad, but you
could

00:15:17.060 --> 00:15:20.197
imagine had that been 10,000 or 20,000 or
30,000.

00:15:20.197 --> 00:15:22.927
So awareness and knowledge probably
reduces

00:15:22.927 --> 00:15:25.656
the number of deaths and probably
constrains

00:15:25.656 --> 00:15:28.528
the misbehavior of these deaths that's
within

00:15:28.528 --> 00:15:31.198
reason, but it doesn't stop the killing.

00:15:31.198 --> 00:15:34.924
That's the question I have to you guys,
or, or to foreign policy.

00:15:34.924 --> 00:15:41.432
Is there any solution to that problem
aside from the traditional foreign policy?

00:15:41.432 --> 00:15:45.960
&gt;&gt; Well the problem then is that it
presents governments that wish to

00:15:45.960 --> 00:15:50.120
show that they are on the side of
international law and will not.

00:15:51.156 --> 00:15:54.819
That there will be serious consequences
for that kind of behavior.

00:15:54.819 --> 00:15:59.370
You're still confronted with the question
of what will those consequences be?

00:15:59.370 --> 00:16:04.861
So technology has in some sense put more
pressure on governments to

00:16:04.861 --> 00:16:10.353
actually act in circumstances in which
they actually have very few

00:16:10.353 --> 00:16:16.120
levers to pull, and I think we're seeing
that in Ukraine today.

00:16:16.120 --> 00:16:22.271
Probably because you see on television
people tweet you, they send out Facebook

00:16:22.271 --> 00:16:28.014
posts, they are showing What is happening
in that square.

00:16:28.014 --> 00:16:32.708
When Czechoslovakia was invaded in 1968 by
the Soviet Union we didn't

00:16:32.708 --> 00:16:36.675
know what was going on on the ground, the
television cameras couldn't

00:16:36.675 --> 00:16:40.244
get in, I was a little girl in Birmingham,
Alabama, I remember

00:16:40.244 --> 00:16:43.780
it pretty well but you couldn't really
tell what was going on.

00:16:43.780 --> 00:16:46.770
Now you know exactly what's going on.

00:16:46.770 --> 00:16:48.420
But you still have to act.

00:16:48.420 --> 00:16:52.610
There's more pressure because you know and
still the tools are not there.

00:16:52.610 --> 00:16:55.990
&gt;&gt; The question I keep asking is that,
sort of what's new?

00:16:55.990 --> 00:16:56.140
Right?

00:16:56.140 --> 00:16:58.451
We've always had disputes, we've always
had bad people,

00:16:58.451 --> 00:17:01.580
we've always had good people, we've always
had governments.

00:17:01.580 --> 00:17:03.734
Jared taught me about this Treaty of
Westphalia.

00:17:03.734 --> 00:17:05.080
&gt;&gt; Yeah, yeah created the states.

00:17:05.080 --> 00:17:06.650
And all, all that kinda stuff.

00:17:06.650 --> 00:17:09.760
&gt;&gt; Stuff that you all, stuff that you all
know.

00:17:09.760 --> 00:17:10.066
I, I did engineering.

00:17:10.066 --> 00:17:10.125
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:17:10.125 --> 00:17:10.360
[LAUGH].

00:17:10.360 --> 00:17:12.130
By the way, the people who created the,
the

00:17:12.130 --> 00:17:15.480
Westphalian system did engineering, too,
of a different sort.

00:17:16.560 --> 00:17:16.950
&gt;&gt; Excellent.

00:17:16.950 --> 00:17:20.740
&gt;&gt; the, so we believe as a group, I think
naively, that if you just empower the

00:17:20.740 --> 00:17:28.901
people with these tools, democracy
flourishes.

00:17:30.340 --> 00:17:33.630
And in the book, we take you through what
happens when you empower people.

00:17:33.630 --> 00:17:37.300
When we were in Myanmar, Burma, we're in
this

00:17:37.300 --> 00:17:40.960
place called Inlay Lake and it's beyond
beautiful and

00:17:40.960 --> 00:17:42.990
some number of kilometers north of us,
there is

00:17:42.990 --> 00:17:46.000
a terrible fight between the Buddhists and
the Muslims.

00:17:46.000 --> 00:17:47.910
And they have a lot of race religious
tensions

00:17:47.910 --> 00:17:50.310
in the country, which I was not aware of.

00:17:50.310 --> 00:17:53.990
And some a whole bunch of houses are, are
burned people are killed.

00:17:53.990 --> 00:17:58.220
And I naively assumed that the internet
had been used a calming mechanism.

00:17:58.220 --> 00:18:01.600
They had just gotten the internet, people
could talk to each other.

00:18:01.600 --> 00:18:03.630
They could see that there's bad you know,
they'd help stop it.

00:18:03.630 --> 00:18:04.900
In fact it was the inverse.

00:18:04.900 --> 00:18:07.625
That the internet was used to inflame
tensions,

00:18:07.625 --> 00:18:09.870
it was used to on both sides, right?

00:18:09.870 --> 00:18:12.800
To get them, they did this they did that,
and so forth.

00:18:13.810 --> 00:18:18.630
Not unlike the radio stuff that occurred
in Rwanda, which we also visited.

00:18:18.630 --> 00:18:22.540
It looks like technology can be used for
both good and bad.

00:18:22.540 --> 00:18:25.920
And, and Jerry made the point that, if
you're an

00:18:25.920 --> 00:18:29.100
early state society, you may not be able
to really critically

00:18:29.100 --> 00:18:32.750
think about these new, information that
you're, so I call this

00:18:32.750 --> 00:18:35.140
a danger zone, and in the book we talked
about this.

00:18:35.140 --> 00:18:37.068
That in these emerging countries, what
happens is, all

00:18:37.068 --> 00:18:39.910
of the sudden, all this connectivity
happens, everyone's empowered.

00:18:39.910 --> 00:18:41.550
And God knows what's gonna happening.

00:18:41.550 --> 00:18:46.790
Because they've not grown up with doubt,
with choices, with different voices,

00:18:46.790 --> 00:18:49.500
and with any ability to choose among those
with some critical [UNKNOWN].

00:18:49.500 --> 00:18:52.987
&gt;&gt; And they've not grown up with
institutions that

00:18:52.987 --> 00:18:58.880
can mediate between differing opinions,
which leads me to.

00:18:58.880 --> 00:19:03.600
Probably the biggest question out there in
terms of the international system.

00:19:03.600 --> 00:19:07.791
And the role of technology and
democratization and the like, China.

00:19:07.791 --> 00:19:12.102
Now Eric, you have some special knowledge
and experience with this.

00:19:12.102 --> 00:19:13.305
Talk a little bit about China.

00:19:13.305 --> 00:19:15.570
Because everyone says China may be sui
generis.

00:19:15.570 --> 00:19:22.066
It may be the one that doesn't respond to
the trends that you are describing.

00:19:22.066 --> 00:19:24.784
&gt;&gt; Well, Jared and I were in, in November,

00:19:24.784 --> 00:19:28.203
in a weekend meeting with the President
and the Prime

00:19:28.203 --> 00:19:32.244
Minister of China with all the mayors
talking about their

00:19:32.244 --> 00:19:37.750
accomplishments and we were among the
Americans that were visiting.

00:19:37.750 --> 00:19:41.140
I think it was remarkable to see the way
they present themselves.

00:19:41.140 --> 00:19:45.560
It's a country of leaders who are
engineers, analytic, numerical.

00:19:45.560 --> 00:19:47.549
They all had their objectives, you know,

00:19:47.549 --> 00:19:50.350
this growth rate, we'll solve this
problem.

00:19:50.350 --> 00:19:53.530
And they spoke with enormous pride of the
lifting of people out

00:19:53.530 --> 00:19:57.410
of abject poverty to what we think of as
lower middle class.

00:19:57.410 --> 00:19:58.910
They understand the middle income trap.

00:19:58.910 --> 00:20:01.740
They're growing at 7.5% and so forth.

00:20:01.740 --> 00:20:05.730
It's a perfect image that they portray
and, and good for them.

00:20:05.730 --> 00:20:08.100
They really have accomplished some amazing
things.

00:20:08.100 --> 00:20:10.808
These are the same people who criminalized
speech

00:20:10.808 --> 00:20:13.323
above 5,000 people by a blogger, that if

00:20:13.323 --> 00:20:15.967
you activate more than 5,000 people in
some

00:20:15.967 --> 00:20:18.629
way, you could literally be arrested and
shot.

00:20:19.676 --> 00:20:24.375
And of course, in China, everyone has
5,000 followers.

00:20:24.375 --> 00:20:25.780
[LAUGH] So it's, just cuz of the scale.

00:20:25.780 --> 00:20:29.690
So it, so it really is a chilling effect.

00:20:29.690 --> 00:20:30.940
How do you rationalize those two?

00:20:30.940 --> 00:20:35.500
Well, one is that American firms are not
welcome, unless they're useful to China.

00:20:35.500 --> 00:20:39.237
Right, so many of the social networks are
blocked, YouTube's blocked, Google,

00:20:39.237 --> 00:20:40.869
after censorship that we thought was

00:20:40.869 --> 00:20:43.470
inappropriate has largely moved to Hong
Kong.

00:20:43.470 --> 00:20:44.350
But they're two things that are

00:20:44.350 --> 00:20:46.975
interesting about China that have emerged
recently.

00:20:46.975 --> 00:20:50.890
Weibo, which is essentially a Twitterish
replacement.

00:20:51.970 --> 00:20:56.050
And WeChat, which today you'd know as
essentially a WhatsApp replacement.

00:20:56.050 --> 00:20:57.520
But what's interesting about these is that

00:20:57.520 --> 00:20:59.370
they're much more than what I've
described.

00:20:59.370 --> 00:21:02.350
They are the ways in which people
communicate online.

00:21:02.350 --> 00:21:04.680
And they are heavily censored.

00:21:04.680 --> 00:21:07.623
Under these very very arbitrary censorship
laws.

00:21:07.623 --> 00:21:09.441
People are in them all the time.

00:21:09.441 --> 00:21:12.093
But I came away with the sense that

00:21:12.093 --> 00:21:16.181
the Chinese government has finally met its
match.

00:21:16.181 --> 00:21:19.284
That if you over here, start talking
through WeChat,

00:21:19.284 --> 00:21:21.750
which is a way of talking to your friends.

00:21:21.750 --> 00:21:24.486
But they're linked together and then
eventually it spreads

00:21:24.486 --> 00:21:26.148
to you all, and then it spreads to you

00:21:26.148 --> 00:21:27.971
all, and then it spreads to you all, and

00:21:27.971 --> 00:21:30.403
it eventually gets to 10 million people a
new idea.

00:21:30.403 --> 00:21:34.726
There's not enough prisons and jails to
arrest all those people.

00:21:34.726 --> 00:21:37.559
So we conclude and it was very clear in
talking to the Chinese

00:21:37.559 --> 00:21:42.220
government that they are, they're at least
aware of this possibility in the internet.

00:21:42.220 --> 00:21:45.138
And they're worried about the internet
being

00:21:45.138 --> 00:21:47.763
used to sort of disturb the social order.

00:21:47.763 --> 00:21:48.106
[CROSSTALK].

00:21:48.106 --> 00:21:49.755
&gt;&gt; We'll see if this, this works.

00:21:49.755 --> 00:21:52.758
I personally believe that as long as they
can

00:21:52.758 --> 00:21:56.040
grow at 7.5%, they can sort of do this.

00:21:56.040 --> 00:22:00.600
At some point, growth slows and then all
of this stuff comes to fore.

00:22:00.600 --> 00:22:03.531
&gt;&gt; Eric, you remember on, on a trip we
took to Beijing, before

00:22:03.531 --> 00:22:08.560
the last one, we talked to a group of, of
business people, journalists, activists.

00:22:08.560 --> 00:22:10.491
And they were very clear about one thing

00:22:10.491 --> 00:22:13.008
that, that, that guides Chinese society
which is

00:22:13.008 --> 00:22:15.642
people don't expect their government to be
honest,

00:22:15.642 --> 00:22:17.710
but they do expect their economy to grow.

00:22:18.740 --> 00:22:21.276
And the moment the economy stops growing
is the moment that the

00:22:21.276 --> 00:22:22.982
lack of honesty, the corruption the

00:22:22.982 --> 00:22:25.060
censorship, all these things start to
matter.

00:22:25.060 --> 00:22:28.350
&gt;&gt; Another example in that same meeting we
were, I, I was

00:22:28.350 --> 00:22:32.502
confused because the data that was
presented was that there was a very

00:22:32.502 --> 00:22:35.797
large environmental activist movement
using the

00:22:35.797 --> 00:22:39.163
internet with actual physical
demonstrations against

00:22:39.163 --> 00:22:43.850
projects and there were a series of such
projects that had been cancelled.

00:22:43.850 --> 00:22:45.896
Right, you, genuine outpourings of civil

00:22:45.896 --> 00:22:48.005
unrest in China which is generally
illegal.

00:22:48.005 --> 00:22:50.018
The answer, and I said to them, this is a

00:22:50.018 --> 00:22:53.220
country that spies on its citizens, they
know your IP address,

00:22:53.220 --> 00:22:56.124
they know who you are, they take
photographs of you, they

00:22:56.124 --> 00:22:59.356
can arbitrarily arrest you, why would a
person take that risk?

00:22:59.356 --> 00:23:00.920
And the answer came back to me.

00:23:00.920 --> 00:23:02.010
They looked me straight in the eye

00:23:02.010 --> 00:23:05.390
and they said, because you're killing my
child.

00:23:07.440 --> 00:23:11.580
There is a limit, there is an absolute
limit, to what

00:23:11.580 --> 00:23:15.620
you can do to a Chinese family in a one
child policy.

00:23:15.620 --> 00:23:21.461
It's interesting that the same principle
did not apply to North Korea.

00:23:21.461 --> 00:23:24.416
That's the difference between having the
internet and not.

00:23:24.416 --> 00:23:24.848
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:23:24.848 --> 00:23:29.483
And the Chinese are an example of
legitimacy based on prosperity.

00:23:29.483 --> 00:23:30.037
Right?

00:23:30.037 --> 00:23:33.612
And the question with prosperity is you
keep chasing it.

00:23:33.612 --> 00:23:37.604
It gets harder and harder because people's
expectations keep going up.

00:23:37.604 --> 00:23:43.608
But I wonder about the role of the of
technology, the internet, information.

00:23:43.608 --> 00:23:45.063
Just information.

00:23:45.063 --> 00:23:50.048
So, for example, pollution in China,
environmental degradation is

00:23:50.048 --> 00:23:53.121
a hot political topic in China and people
can

00:23:53.121 --> 00:23:56.859
walk outside of their apartments or
wherever and they

00:23:56.859 --> 00:24:01.200
know they can't breathe in Shanghai or
Chengdu or wherever.

00:24:02.500 --> 00:24:04.140
And for a long time the government was
giving a

00:24:04.140 --> 00:24:09.630
pollution index number that clearly didn't
bear any resemblance to reality.

00:24:09.630 --> 00:24:14.920
The US Embassy started publishing a number
or putting a number up but there's also

00:24:14.920 --> 00:24:21.470
now apparently an app that you can buy
that will measure the pollutants.

00:24:21.470 --> 00:24:26.930
So just the provision of information
challenges the monopoly on information

00:24:26.930 --> 00:24:32.710
that an authoritarian government depends
on for control and acquiescence.

00:24:32.710 --> 00:24:34.680
Do you agree with that?

00:24:34.680 --> 00:24:36.940
&gt;&gt; And in China this is going to be a
particular challenge.

00:24:36.940 --> 00:24:39.592
So lots of people like to make the
argument that China

00:24:39.592 --> 00:24:43.812
has a very sophisticated censorship
apparatus, but in reality it, China's

00:24:43.812 --> 00:24:46.705
about to go through an experiment that no
other country in

00:24:46.705 --> 00:24:50.130
history will ever grow through and it'll
happen just one time.

00:24:50.130 --> 00:24:51.980
Which is a billion people are gonna come
online

00:24:51.980 --> 00:24:53.770
in one country in the span of a decade.

00:24:53.770 --> 00:24:56.020
And you have to ask yourself the question,
who are they?

00:24:56.020 --> 00:24:58.178
They are largely rural.

00:24:58.178 --> 00:25:01.482
Often in the Western province, they have
no visibility into

00:25:01.482 --> 00:25:04.867
what's going on in the various urban
environments in China.

00:25:04.867 --> 00:25:08.533
And so, the reason this is game-changing
is the grievance of one

00:25:08.533 --> 00:25:12.210
city has the potential to scale to a
grievance across all cities.

00:25:12.210 --> 00:25:14.570
It's that sort of visibility into other

00:25:14.570 --> 00:25:17.580
Chinese communities' problems and
challenges and grievances.

00:25:17.580 --> 00:25:22.480
And so, the challenge of these complaints
and these frustrations scaling.

00:25:22.480 --> 00:25:26.360
Even the Chinese themselves have no idea
what that, that looks like.

00:25:26.360 --> 00:25:27.830
It's, it's a huge wild card for them.

00:25:27.830 --> 00:25:30.713
The other thing that they're doing, just
to do everything

00:25:30.713 --> 00:25:34.720
they can to hedge their monopoly on
information, is they're literally.

00:25:34.720 --> 00:25:36.800
Trying to build spheres of cyber-influence
by

00:25:36.800 --> 00:25:40.090
building out the technological
infrastructure of other countries.

00:25:40.090 --> 00:25:43.100
Because they have a blurry line between
public and private sector.

00:25:43.100 --> 00:25:45.110
It's their own form of foreign assistance
that no

00:25:45.110 --> 00:25:47.260
other country seems to be able to compete
with.

00:25:47.260 --> 00:25:49.314
&gt;&gt; One of the things that we have talked

00:25:49.314 --> 00:25:52.329
about internally and I think it's fair to
say.

00:25:53.340 --> 00:25:55.721
Is that there are solutions to his level

00:25:55.721 --> 00:25:59.258
of censorship, a combination of public key
cryptography

00:25:59.258 --> 00:26:04.400
and a whole bunch of new inventions which
we can talk about if you're interested in.

00:26:04.400 --> 00:26:08.347
Make it possible to imagine that in five
years maybe a decade, it

00:26:08.347 --> 00:26:14.130
will be impossible for countries like
China to keep their citizens in the dark.

00:26:14.130 --> 00:26:17.270
They would have to rely on other means of
oppression.

00:26:18.450 --> 00:26:21.520
But the traditional blocking the
information through a firewall or

00:26:21.520 --> 00:26:25.790
restricting access looks to us like we can
stop that.

00:26:25.790 --> 00:26:28.264
&gt;&gt; I'm going to ask that we get ready now

00:26:28.264 --> 00:26:32.509
for the audience participation part of the
discussion, but I

00:26:32.509 --> 00:26:35.765
wanna close with one last question which
brings us back

00:26:35.765 --> 00:26:39.540
from China, North Korea and soon to the
United States.

00:26:39.540 --> 00:26:43.251
One of the most controversial issues that
we face here here in

00:26:43.251 --> 00:26:48.620
the United States where we do have strong
democratic institutions and the like.

00:26:48.620 --> 00:26:53.352
It's been the question about privacy,
technology, security.

00:26:53.352 --> 00:26:56.106
The fight against terrorism and the like.

00:26:56.106 --> 00:26:59.308
That has been the subject of discussion
about

00:26:59.308 --> 00:27:02.706
the role of surveillance, the role of NSA.

00:27:02.706 --> 00:27:06.673
the, the Snowdens, the Bradley Mannings of
the world.

00:27:06.673 --> 00:27:12.855
How do we think about the impact of those
events on how governments may view

00:27:12.855 --> 00:27:19.752
technology how citizens may view it, and
the relationship between the two.

00:27:19.752 --> 00:27:23.183
&gt;&gt; Well let's, let's distinguish between
governments

00:27:23.183 --> 00:27:26.473
and companies, and let's talk about
governments.

00:27:26.473 --> 00:27:31.650
I worry about governments collecting large
amounts of data on their citizens.

00:27:32.840 --> 00:27:36.910
And not for the reasons that all the nice
civil libertarians in the room think.

00:27:36.910 --> 00:27:39.240
I worry because those large databases will
be leaked.

00:27:40.730 --> 00:27:43.314
Because one of the other things that's
new, is

00:27:43.314 --> 00:27:46.730
the ability to do this large scale bulk
data leaking.

00:27:46.730 --> 00:27:49.954
It used to be if you looked at the
previous leaks they were essentially

00:27:49.954 --> 00:27:54.020
limited by the rate at which you could
make Xerox copies, you know, of things.

00:27:54.020 --> 00:27:56.690
This is sort of a physical component.

00:27:56.690 --> 00:28:04.899
But it appears now that it's possible to
steal 1.5 plus or minus million documents

00:28:04.899 --> 00:28:11.610
with a little bit of work from Hawaii, and
then put that around the world.

00:28:12.900 --> 00:28:15.615
This is really a new problem.

00:28:15.615 --> 00:28:20.730
And governments naturally collect data on
their citizens.

00:28:20.730 --> 00:28:25.350
Current ver, curr, the, current that
every, your phone knows exactly where you

00:28:25.350 --> 00:28:29.913
are because the E911 services are
required, by law, to record where you are.

00:28:29.913 --> 00:28:33.114
Now thank goodness that information has
not been leaked,

00:28:33.114 --> 00:28:36.830
but the fact of the matter is, the
databases exist.

00:28:36.830 --> 00:28:40.470
I'm not particularly sure I think, we want
that stuff leaked, generally

00:28:40.470 --> 00:28:44.390
available and so forth, let alone
subpoenaed, used in courts and so forth.

00:28:44.390 --> 00:28:50.260
Now, in the, we debated the Snowden case
at some length, traditional foreign policy

00:28:50.260 --> 00:28:53.690
people, and people, especially in the East
Coast, are very, very negative on Sonwden.

00:28:53.690 --> 00:28:56.260
The West Coast tends to be more.

00:28:57.590 --> 00:28:59.810
More shall we say positive and the view

00:28:59.810 --> 00:29:03.760
of student appears to have changed over
the year.

00:29:03.760 --> 00:29:08.040
To be somewhat more positive from an
initial very negative view.

00:29:08.040 --> 00:29:11.448
But the fact of the matter is, I just
don't think we

00:29:11.448 --> 00:29:16.590
want to encourage vault data leaking, even
if it had a good purpose.

00:29:16.590 --> 00:29:19.730
Who appointed Mr. Assange or Mr. Snowden
be the person who made that decision.

00:29:19.730 --> 00:29:21.230
It's not a good way to run your society.

00:29:21.230 --> 00:29:24.560
So in the book we basically say there's
gotta be

00:29:26.620 --> 00:29:29.210
some other way to deal with government
misbehavior and so forth.

00:29:29.210 --> 00:29:32.860
But the other thing we say very clearly
and

00:29:32.860 --> 00:29:37.410
indeed in the current issues around the
NSA surveillance.

00:29:37.410 --> 00:29:39.880
I'm happy that we know that the government
was doing this.

00:29:39.880 --> 00:29:42.780
I think in democracy you should roughly
know what the government was doing.

00:29:42.780 --> 00:29:44.730
It's not really a good idea for them to be
collecting all

00:29:44.730 --> 00:29:47.080
this information, because somebody else
will leak some other part of it.

00:29:48.190 --> 00:29:51.436
&gt;&gt; And, and just to add to that, there's a
few other dimensions to this.

00:29:51.436 --> 00:29:54.413
Eric and I, when the, in research for the
hardcover version

00:29:54.413 --> 00:29:57.410
of the book, we spent five and a half
hours interviewing.

00:29:57.410 --> 00:30:01.159
Julian Assange well, he was under house
arrest in, in, in the UK.

00:30:01.159 --> 00:30:04.932
Trying to understand technologically what
he was trying to build and

00:30:04.932 --> 00:30:07.804
what he thinks he might be able to build
in ten years.

00:30:07.804 --> 00:30:10.885
And we came to the conclusion that Eric
mentioned, which is-.

00:30:10.885 --> 00:30:14.525
You know the problem is who are, who are
these people to determine that they're

00:30:14.525 --> 00:30:18.660
the ones anointed to make a decision about
what should be public and what shouldn't?

00:30:18.660 --> 00:30:21.058
But it's actually even more complicated
than that with

00:30:21.058 --> 00:30:24.272
vault leaking, because there's nobody,
there's no way any individual

00:30:24.272 --> 00:30:26.261
can read all of these documents and even
make an

00:30:26.261 --> 00:30:29.138
informed judgement about what kind of harm
they're gonna cause.

00:30:29.138 --> 00:30:32.951
&gt;&gt; So we actually wrote our position in
the book, and you can, you'll read it.

00:30:32.951 --> 00:30:37.012
And from the safety of his new residence
in London,

00:30:37.012 --> 00:30:41.671
Mr. Sanj wrote a scathing critique of us
and our view.

00:30:41.671 --> 00:30:45.213
Saying that we were puppet masters for the
tech elite.

00:30:45.213 --> 00:30:46.481
&gt;&gt; And of course he leaked our interview.

00:30:46.481 --> 00:30:47.912
&gt;&gt; And he leaked our interview.

00:30:47.912 --> 00:30:50.404
So, which you can also read online.

00:30:50.404 --> 00:30:53.125
&gt;&gt; What, what did you expect?

00:30:53.125 --> 00:30:53.240
[LAUGH] yeah.

00:30:53.240 --> 00:30:55.712
&gt;&gt; So, so, my, my point here is I think
this

00:30:55.712 --> 00:31:00.194
information is being gathered, maybe for
good purposes or maybe for not.

00:31:00.194 --> 00:31:04.117
But if you're going to gather that
information, you better have

00:31:04.117 --> 00:31:07.720
a really good idea of how it's not gonna
get stolen.

00:31:07.720 --> 00:31:09.430
It's not gonna get [INAUDIBLE] leaked.

00:31:09.430 --> 00:31:12.520
And it's gonna be protected, or maybe you
shouldn't do it at all.

00:31:12.520 --> 00:31:14.110
And I think that's ultimately, I think
[INAUDIBLE].

00:31:14.110 --> 00:31:17.530
&gt;&gt; Let me just, but, but of course, it's
not just the problem with government.

00:31:17.530 --> 00:31:19.330
Lots of people collect data on you.

00:31:19.330 --> 00:31:21.640
As a matter of fact, I would dare say,
Amazon probably

00:31:21.640 --> 00:31:24.860
knows more about you than the US
government and they care more.

00:31:24.860 --> 00:31:25.255
All right?

00:31:25.255 --> 00:31:29.666
Because the NSA actually doesn't care what
you said to your grandmother.

00:31:29.666 --> 00:31:31.526
Amazon does, [LAUGH] because you might buy

00:31:31.526 --> 00:31:33.981
something that you discussed with your
grandmother.

00:31:33.981 --> 00:31:37.085
So, sometimes I think we have to recognize
that one of the

00:31:37.085 --> 00:31:40.848
problems of your point about fault data
leaking is an important one.

00:31:40.848 --> 00:31:44.200
But democracy is always right at the edge
of chaos.

00:31:44.200 --> 00:31:48.375
And it's a disruptive system where people
can say anything.

00:31:48.375 --> 00:31:49.926
It's a disruptive system where

00:31:49.926 --> 00:31:52.630
we're always over throwing governments
peacefully.

00:31:52.630 --> 00:31:59.316
And we depend on institutions to moderate
and mediate those disruptions.

00:31:59.316 --> 00:32:01.856
And if individual citizens are deciding,

00:32:01.856 --> 00:32:04.748
rather than the institutions of
government, in

00:32:04.748 --> 00:32:06.439
the case of the United States

00:32:06.439 --> 00:32:10.196
there're three separate co-equal branches
of government.

00:32:10.196 --> 00:32:12.820
Then you've got a chaotic environment and
I think

00:32:12.820 --> 00:32:15.515
that is part of the problem with the, the
Snowden.

00:32:15.515 --> 00:32:17.927
&gt;&gt; Well also, and then you add the
celebrity factor to this, right?

00:32:17.927 --> 00:32:18.666
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:32:18.666 --> 00:32:21.844
&gt;&gt; I mean, and, and that to me, if you
look at the progression of

00:32:21.844 --> 00:32:23.863
leakers in the past and then Manning and

00:32:23.863 --> 00:32:26.617
then Snowden, these people are becoming
more famous

00:32:26.617 --> 00:32:28.451
over the time, over time, and as we

00:32:28.451 --> 00:32:31.695
talked about backstage, it lends itself
towards copycatting

00:32:31.695 --> 00:32:33.530
and I think your point about this not

00:32:33.530 --> 00:32:36.140
just being a government issue is also
important.

00:32:36.140 --> 00:32:38.580
What happens the day that somebody at a
major law firm

00:32:38.580 --> 00:32:41.644
in the United States decides to bulk leak
a bunch of information?

00:32:41.644 --> 00:32:44.106
What, what are the, what are the
consequences of that?

00:32:44.106 --> 00:32:46.158
And then there's another issue about what
all of

00:32:46.158 --> 00:32:49.284
this means for the future of the
intelligence community.

00:32:49.284 --> 00:32:52.599
One of my, my mentors is, is, is Frank
Carluci, who

00:32:52.599 --> 00:32:56.160
used to be deputy director of the CIA
among other things.

00:32:56.160 --> 00:32:59.472
And he said to me in what must have been
10 years ago that

00:32:59.472 --> 00:33:04.560
the challenge the CIA has is it's becoming
increasingly difficult to keep a secret.

00:33:04.560 --> 00:33:05.980
And that was decade ago.

00:33:05.980 --> 00:33:08.824
So the intelligence community has a real
challenge that

00:33:08.824 --> 00:33:11.002
it has to overcome, which is how it, how
it

00:33:11.002 --> 00:33:13.180
continues to do its good work in an era
where

00:33:13.180 --> 00:33:16.047
more and more there's a risk of things
getting leaked.

00:33:16.047 --> 00:33:18.350
And, I, I don't know the answer to that
question.

00:33:18.350 --> 00:33:18.979
&gt;&gt; All right.

00:33:18.979 --> 00:33:22.199
We're now gonna go to the audience
participation.

00:33:22.199 --> 00:33:24.549
&gt;&gt; You talked about that you were worried
about the country

00:33:24.549 --> 00:33:27.261
stealing your data and more worried about
you stealing my data.

00:33:27.261 --> 00:33:30.238
And the point is that the government shuts
down for 7

00:33:30.238 --> 00:33:33.850
days nobody cares, Google goes down for 5
minutes the world stops.

00:33:33.850 --> 00:33:34.559
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:33:34.559 --> 00:33:40.526
&gt;&gt; And so the thing which I would like to
ask you is, I control my government.

00:33:40.526 --> 00:33:42.506
I know that I am electing this president.

00:33:42.506 --> 00:33:44.949
I don't control the Google CEO chairman.

00:33:44.949 --> 00:33:48.324
Should there be elections for people who
steal, who, control our data?

00:33:48.324 --> 00:33:51.177
[LAUGH] And how do you bring
accountability [INAUDIBLE]?

00:33:51.177 --> 00:33:51.568
Thank you.

00:33:51.568 --> 00:33:52.245
&gt;&gt; Very good for you.

00:33:52.245 --> 00:33:52.644
[UNKNOWN].

00:33:52.644 --> 00:33:53.649
&gt;&gt; I was care, I was careful with my
answer

00:33:53.649 --> 00:33:55.580
to say that you wanna distinguish between
governments and corporations.

00:33:59.640 --> 00:34:03.470
Governments have a monopoly on the, the
use of force.

00:34:03.470 --> 00:34:06.930
They can actually send the police, they
can shoot you, they can put in jail.

00:34:06.930 --> 00:34:09.870
None of those tools are available to
corporations.

00:34:09.870 --> 00:34:14.064
And indeed, companies are subject to
numerous laws, regulations, and so forth.

00:34:14.064 --> 00:34:16.450
Not just in the US, but everywhere else.

00:34:16.450 --> 00:34:20.338
But the ultimate reason for you not to be
too worried about

00:34:20.338 --> 00:34:24.882
us, is that we depend on, at some lev,
basic level, your trust.

00:34:24.882 --> 00:34:26.961
And if there were a data breach at

00:34:26.961 --> 00:34:31.427
Google, involving anyone, it would
immediately become front page

00:34:31.427 --> 00:34:34.507
news all around the world and millions of
people

00:34:34.507 --> 00:34:37.838
would say, screw that, we're gonna go use
Bing.

00:34:37.838 --> 00:34:38.800
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Right.

00:34:38.800 --> 00:34:40.030
Seriously.

00:34:40.030 --> 00:34:43.035
So, which is, is clearly not a good thing.

00:34:43.035 --> 00:34:45.776
[LAUGH] So, so ultimately, the answer to

00:34:45.776 --> 00:34:49.253
your question is in a competitive market,
right?

00:34:49.253 --> 00:34:52.942
The competition, plus the regulations,
plus the fear

00:34:52.942 --> 00:34:56.632
of regulations, plus the, the serious
threat of civil

00:34:56.632 --> 00:35:00.008
and criminal penalties, which are in fact,
often

00:35:00.008 --> 00:35:03.639
applied to CEO's and chairmen are your
best defense.

00:35:03.639 --> 00:35:09.917
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:35:09.917 --> 00:35:11.490
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Okay, all right.

00:35:11.490 --> 00:35:14.330
Hi, my name is Malin, I'm a senior at
Stanford.

00:35:14.330 --> 00:35:16.225
I think there's sort of a large current

00:35:16.225 --> 00:35:18.635
of dissatisfaction with the government
here in Silicon

00:35:18.635 --> 00:35:20.788
Valley, and you also spoke about
technology sort

00:35:20.788 --> 00:35:23.940
of as a way around traditional foreign
policy mechanisms.

00:35:23.940 --> 00:35:27.910
But in the end, Google is part of America,
Google's part of a democracy.

00:35:27.910 --> 00:35:32.720
So how do you view Google's civic
responsibility both here and, and abroad?

00:35:32.720 --> 00:35:37.243
&gt;&gt; You know, I had a very interesting mind
shift that I had to experience when I

00:35:37.243 --> 00:35:39.986
went from the state department to Google,
because

00:35:39.986 --> 00:35:43.060
I was used to talking about our foreign
policy.

00:35:43.060 --> 00:35:46.240
And I got to Google and everybody told me
there was no Google foreign policy.

00:35:46.240 --> 00:35:50.790
It's a global company that's defined by a
mission and a set of values.

00:35:50.790 --> 00:35:53.500
and, and, that's a really different way of

00:35:53.500 --> 00:35:55.620
thinking about things than, than when
you're in government.

00:35:55.620 --> 00:36:00.141
So, when you part of the reason, at, at
Google we've taken on this mission

00:36:00.141 --> 00:36:04.395
of, of, of free expression, is it's
something that makes sense for us as a

00:36:04.395 --> 00:36:08.715
company, and it's an issue that we can get
behind that might sort of inch

00:36:08.715 --> 00:36:10.243
right up to the line of being

00:36:10.243 --> 00:36:14.330
political without favoring, say, one
specific government, right?

00:36:14.330 --> 00:36:17.130
And Google as a company, we stand by the
free flow of information

00:36:17.130 --> 00:36:21.230
and we should stand by that everywhere
from the United States to Pyongyang.

00:36:21.230 --> 00:36:24.604
But it's, you know, there is this miss,
you know, just because Google is

00:36:24.604 --> 00:36:28.420
headquartered in the US doesn't mean that
it is exclusively a, a, a US company.

00:36:28.420 --> 00:36:30.775
&gt;&gt; Goo, Google's basic answer is we are in
favor of

00:36:30.775 --> 00:36:34.480
an open and free internet for every human
being on the planet.

00:36:34.480 --> 00:36:36.763
And remember that that strategy's also
self serving

00:36:36.763 --> 00:36:39.502
to our shareholders because eventually
they will become Google

00:36:39.502 --> 00:36:42.294
users and eventually they'll have
monetization and eventually

00:36:42.294 --> 00:36:45.404
they'll have credit cards and eventually
they'll have advertising.

00:36:45.404 --> 00:36:48.726
And because we have the benefit of time,
we're willing to wait for the

00:36:48.726 --> 00:36:52.860
20 years or 30 years for those consumers
to become rabid Google advertising fans.

00:36:52.860 --> 00:36:56.473
So it's in our self interest, but it's
also the right thing to do morally.

00:36:56.473 --> 00:36:58.747
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:36:58.747 --> 00:37:03.961
So my name is [UNKNOWN] actually maybe,
one of the

00:37:03.961 --> 00:37:10.049
victims of the technology was involved in
the Egyptian Revolution.

00:37:10.049 --> 00:37:14.803
And it's amazing what you said you know,
it's just you said, what's on

00:37:14.803 --> 00:37:20.011
my, what I wanted to say is there's a lot
of responsibility to technology.

00:37:20.011 --> 00:37:23.668
You know, I mean the revolution happened
we didn't expect.

00:37:23.668 --> 00:37:26.341
You know, the regime is going to change

00:37:26.341 --> 00:37:30.181
it's been 3 years what happened is the
aftermath.

00:37:30.181 --> 00:37:36.060
I feel there is a lot of responsibility on
us to to actually have this work.

00:37:36.060 --> 00:37:41.300
And there's responsibility maybe on you,
to help us make this work.

00:37:41.300 --> 00:37:47.160
And so and, I, I started an NGO, I moved
to one of the largest slum areas in Egypt.

00:37:47.160 --> 00:37:52.460
I actually had a visit from one of their
VPs, Mohammed Godet, what an amazing guy.

00:37:52.460 --> 00:37:57.022
And I felt that why not recreate a, a
successful model?

00:37:57.022 --> 00:37:57.940
Right?

00:37:57.940 --> 00:38:02.426
Of revolution or revolutions that are
happening, or technology that

00:38:02.426 --> 00:38:06.250
makes a big change and is sustainable and,
and continues.

00:38:06.250 --> 00:38:09.080
So it's not a matter of maybe okay you
[INAUDIBLE] you travel

00:38:09.080 --> 00:38:12.083
around the world, but why don't we know
keep on and following

00:38:12.083 --> 00:38:14.273
up, I mean if you were left out, I mean
most of

00:38:14.273 --> 00:38:18.080
the evolutionaries now are in jail or
[LAUGH] they're outside of the country.

00:38:18.080 --> 00:38:22.243
But why, I mean why some, Google that was
so interested and the whole world was like

00:38:22.243 --> 00:38:24.562
with us and now they're like you know,
just,

00:38:24.562 --> 00:38:27.570
just let's look to another part of the
world.

00:38:27.570 --> 00:38:30.600
Although, there's still much that can be
done, you know, to

00:38:30.600 --> 00:38:33.288
follow up on this and actually make this
a, a successful

00:38:33.288 --> 00:38:36.375
story that can be replicated, you know, in
other, other youth

00:38:36.375 --> 00:38:39.206
who are going to take the lead and change
their countries.

00:38:39.206 --> 00:38:40.693
Thanks.

00:38:40.693 --> 00:38:41.250
&gt;&gt; Arthur?

00:38:41.250 --> 00:38:43.100
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I mean, our, our belief and we
write

00:38:43.100 --> 00:38:45.350
this in the book technology has an
empowerment by us.

00:38:45.350 --> 00:38:45.698
Right?

00:38:45.698 --> 00:38:49.395
It obviously you know, empowers people to
do extraordinary things, but when

00:38:49.395 --> 00:38:52.710
I look at the situation in Egypt, you,
you, you see two challenges.

00:38:52.710 --> 00:38:56.466
A lack of institutions that can function
and deliver basic goods and social

00:38:56.466 --> 00:39:00.222
services to the population and two, you
know, it's very important if you

00:39:00.222 --> 00:39:04.156
start a revolution to make sure that that
revolution produces new leaders with

00:39:04.156 --> 00:39:08.730
new last names who can deliver better than
the autocrat that you overthrew.

00:39:08.730 --> 00:39:09.945
And it takes time, right?

00:39:09.945 --> 00:39:13.518
So one of the challenges, you talked about
expectations, you know,

00:39:13.518 --> 00:39:17.322
technology does raise expectations and
it's very easy for people to galvanize

00:39:17.322 --> 00:39:20.444
around the lowest common denominator which
is, we don't like an

00:39:20.444 --> 00:39:23.700
autocrat that shut down our networks,
let's get him out of power.

00:39:23.700 --> 00:39:25.979
But that's basically the only thing that
anybody agrees on.

00:39:25.979 --> 00:39:27.313
And so the [CROSSTALK] go ahead Erick.

00:39:27.313 --> 00:39:29.351
&gt;&gt; In the book we actually tell the story

00:39:29.351 --> 00:39:32.110
of Vodaphone, and as you know, to hear a
square,

00:39:32.110 --> 00:39:34.675
they actually shut down the internet for 4
and

00:39:34.675 --> 00:39:37.472
a half years, 4 and a half days, excuse
me.

00:39:37.472 --> 00:39:40.970
And they sent out fake SMS messages which
were so stupid, everyone

00:39:40.970 --> 00:39:45.410
figured out that they were coming from the
Ministry of Propaganda, under Mubarak.

00:39:46.780 --> 00:39:52.192
When they shut down the internet, the
average person in, in the, in Egypt all

00:39:52.192 --> 00:39:57.442
of a sudden got a, a definitive message
that the government was terrified.

00:39:57.442 --> 00:40:01.420
And people's opinion shifted in favor of
the protests.

00:40:01.420 --> 00:40:04.052
So the internet was a, a key component of

00:40:04.052 --> 00:40:07.227
the, of the first part of the revolution,
but it

00:40:07.227 --> 00:40:10.868
provided very little help in the second
part, which was

00:40:10.868 --> 00:40:16.480
identifying real leaders doing the
political negotiation, building the teams.

00:40:16.480 --> 00:40:18.762
I think it's fair to say that Ukraine
which has

00:40:18.762 --> 00:40:21.989
many problems unrelated to the internet,
has the same problem.

00:40:21.989 --> 00:40:22.290
Right?

00:40:22.290 --> 00:40:24.522
The, the, the guy that they didn't like
has now

00:40:24.522 --> 00:40:27.130
fled the country, they can't agree on a
new leader.

00:40:27.130 --> 00:40:31.225
But now you've got all of these people who
are very sophisticated, very

00:40:31.225 --> 00:40:34.584
internet connected, saying, where's my
government and

00:40:34.584 --> 00:40:37.221
why haven't you fixed everything right
now?

00:40:37.221 --> 00:40:40.603
So pity the revolutionary leader, who
takes this

00:40:40.603 --> 00:40:44.316
emotionally charged thing and has no time
to operate.

00:40:44.316 --> 00:40:45.455
And that's the fault of the internet.

00:40:45.455 --> 00:40:47.266
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:40:47.266 --> 00:40:48.470
Okay?

00:40:48.470 --> 00:40:50.875
&gt;&gt; Yes, if, if I could just on, on this
point though,

00:40:50.875 --> 00:40:53.800
it, it's also the case that these things
really do take time.

00:40:54.834 --> 00:40:58.880
No revolution on the day after is actually
successful.

00:40:58.880 --> 00:41:00.621
That's the thing to remember.

00:41:00.621 --> 00:41:03.444
From the French Revolution, the American
Revolution,

00:41:03.444 --> 00:41:05.576
no revolution is successful the next day.

00:41:05.576 --> 00:41:09.967
Because technology I think is an
accelerant of underlying trends.

00:41:09.967 --> 00:41:13.952
It actually is neither a cause of what
happens after,

00:41:13.952 --> 00:41:17.330
nor is it a solution to what happens after
it.

00:41:17.330 --> 00:41:20.710
And so, the question is can it be part of
a solution going forward?

00:41:20.710 --> 00:41:23.850
And I think that's really the interesting
question

00:41:23.850 --> 00:41:28.510
for those who, who promote the optimistic
technological future.

00:41:28.510 --> 00:41:28.800
Let's see.

00:41:28.800 --> 00:41:30.550
Where is the next question?

00:41:30.550 --> 00:41:31.280
Okay, yes.

00:41:31.280 --> 00:41:33.648
&gt;&gt; Hi, my name is Tim I'm an undergrad as
well.

00:41:33.648 --> 00:41:37.257
My question has to do with the ways we can
use technology to change

00:41:37.257 --> 00:41:39.811
institutions in the western world are
generally

00:41:39.811 --> 00:41:42.942
you could call a good governments, right?

00:41:42.942 --> 00:41:46.412
And the question is that historically we
relied on a very strong

00:41:46.412 --> 00:41:51.260
signal about public opinion every 4 years,
or whatever the election cycle is.

00:41:51.260 --> 00:41:55.110
We can imagine technologies nowadays
allowing decision-makers to get a

00:41:55.110 --> 00:41:59.322
very strong signal about what its populace
wants quite immediately.

00:41:59.322 --> 00:42:00.754
How do you think governments can

00:42:00.754 --> 00:42:03.459
harness that technology in their
decision-making process

00:42:03.459 --> 00:42:05.421
and how should we change institutions
given

00:42:05.421 --> 00:42:07.500
that we have this technology for
government?

00:42:08.710 --> 00:42:11.882
&gt;&gt; Well, I think, I mean, the, the natural
thing that technology does to

00:42:11.882 --> 00:42:13.598
politics, say right here in the United

00:42:13.598 --> 00:42:15.630
States, is it makes it all more
transparent.

00:42:15.630 --> 00:42:17.290
Now, that also comes at, at a cost.

00:42:17.290 --> 00:42:20.160
It generates a lot more, a lot, lot more
noise.

00:42:20.160 --> 00:42:23.620
I mean I, I think, you know, this isn't a,
a, an answer

00:42:23.620 --> 00:42:27.490
to your specific question, but I think
there, there's a fundamental problem in

00:42:27.490 --> 00:42:31.480
the American system that you can relate to
technology, which is, it's scaring

00:42:31.480 --> 00:42:35.190
all the young people and future leaders
from ever wanting to go into government.

00:42:35.190 --> 00:42:37.870
Because everybody posted stupid things
online, at an age

00:42:37.870 --> 00:42:40.720
before they were aware of the long term
consequences.

00:42:40.720 --> 00:42:42.352
And so, there is a risk, you know, we,

00:42:42.352 --> 00:42:44.804
we're here at Stanford, smartest people in
the world.

00:42:44.804 --> 00:42:46.224
I'm not just saying that 'cuz I went
there.

00:42:46.224 --> 00:42:47.539
[LAUGH] Speaking as a Stanford graduate.

00:42:47.539 --> 00:42:49.302
[LAUGH] You know, I, I, it's.

00:42:49.302 --> 00:42:51.254
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] I knew we'd get back at Cal
sooner or later during this conversation.

00:42:51.254 --> 00:42:53.958
&gt;&gt; [CROSSTALK] But there is, there is a
responsibility that

00:42:53.958 --> 00:42:56.601
all the students here has, have as people
who are gonna

00:42:56.601 --> 00:42:59.073
graduate and go on to, to lead the world
is there's

00:42:59.073 --> 00:43:02.313
a responsibility to make sure it's not led
by the leftovers.

00:43:02.313 --> 00:43:05.505
And that means that there do, there do
have to be a set of brave young

00:43:05.505 --> 00:43:07.671
people who decide, you know what, maybe I,

00:43:07.671 --> 00:43:10.008
you know, maybe there's a risk associated
with

00:43:10.008 --> 00:43:13.828
a life filled with transparency, but you
know, we're gonna be a part of the few

00:43:13.828 --> 00:43:15.766
brave souls that get out there and decide

00:43:15.766 --> 00:43:18.470
that, despite the consequences of that we
wanna lead.

00:43:18.470 --> 00:43:20.305
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE] could I, could I just, on
this

00:43:20.305 --> 00:43:22.672
point though, because I, I wanna make an
argument.

00:43:22.672 --> 00:43:27.069
A provocative argument, that actually,
this is a place that technology is

00:43:27.069 --> 00:43:32.030
partially to blame for the institutional
gridlock that we see, in Washington.

00:43:32.030 --> 00:43:34.420
And actually, it's true in lots of
democracies.

00:43:34.420 --> 00:43:35.740
But let me make

00:43:37.890 --> 00:43:38.560
the American case.

00:43:38.560 --> 00:43:39.450
I'm gonna date myself now.

00:43:39.450 --> 00:43:41.450
But when I was a kid, I grew up,

00:43:41.450 --> 00:43:46.410
watching the Huntley-Brinkley Report with
my parents every night.

00:43:46.410 --> 00:43:47.550
That's how we got our news.

00:43:47.550 --> 00:43:49.210
Some people watched Walter Cronkite.

00:43:49.210 --> 00:43:50.660
Some people watched Coward K.

00:43:50.660 --> 00:43:51.040
Smith.

00:43:51.040 --> 00:43:52.160
But that's how we got our news.

00:43:53.190 --> 00:43:56.580
Now, and so basically, we had the same
news source.

00:43:56.580 --> 00:44:02.720
Now I can go to my blog, my cable news
network, my aggregation sites,

00:44:02.720 --> 00:44:05.837
and I can never actually encounter the

00:44:05.837 --> 00:44:10.529
views and opinions of somebody who thinks
differently.

00:44:10.529 --> 00:44:11.510
Right?

00:44:11.510 --> 00:44:15.368
So we vulcanized information news in a way
that I

00:44:15.368 --> 00:44:19.232
only have to listen to people who think
like me.

00:44:19.232 --> 00:44:22.350
When you only listen to people who think
like you, you

00:44:22.350 --> 00:44:26.248
think people who don't think like you are
either venal or stupid.

00:44:26.248 --> 00:44:30.668
And that then gets reflected in the
polarization of politics in Washington.

00:44:30.668 --> 00:44:35.818
So, actually both the speed of technology,
and its

00:44:35.818 --> 00:44:41.800
balkanization has contributed to the
inability to govern.

00:44:41.800 --> 00:44:43.575
How do you react [CROSSTALK] to that?

00:44:43.575 --> 00:44:47.147
&gt;&gt; I think we would, we completely agree
with what you said, and I would in,

00:44:47.147 --> 00:44:49.338
to further support it, I, I'm good friends

00:44:49.338 --> 00:44:52.500
with a, a Republican Senator from the
South.

00:44:52.500 --> 00:44:55.029
And I'm visiting him, and he had just

00:44:55.029 --> 00:44:58.046
narrowly escaped a brush with the tea
party.

00:44:58.046 --> 00:45:00.018
And I said, how was the campaign?

00:45:00.018 --> 00:45:02.941
And he said that it got so bad that he, on
his

00:45:02.941 --> 00:45:07.001
mirror in the morning at home, he shaves,
and he wrote in,

00:45:07.001 --> 00:45:11.788
in lipstick or whatever you write on, on
mirrors [LAUGH] There are

00:45:11.788 --> 00:45:15.661
camera's everywhere, everyone is watching
you,

00:45:15.661 --> 00:45:19.366
you're always recorded, keep your mouth
shut.

00:45:19.366 --> 00:45:20.635
Okay.

00:45:20.635 --> 00:45:22.191
This is a senator.

00:45:22.191 --> 00:45:24.750
It's not some random person.

00:45:24.750 --> 00:45:25.920
And I worry that, it's, the way to answer

00:45:25.920 --> 00:45:30.590
your question is, what do we worry about
in democracy.

00:45:31.790 --> 00:45:34.859
And I worry that there's been a loss of
what I would

00:45:34.859 --> 00:45:38.150
call deliberation, in deliberative
thinking there

00:45:38.150 --> 00:45:40.269
are, there are some problems that

00:45:40.269 --> 00:45:45.606
cannot be solved in an hour or a day, they
require sophisticated political leadership

00:45:45.606 --> 00:45:50.880
lots of time investment, lots of
discussion sometimes behind closed doors.

00:45:50.880 --> 00:45:53.020
And we're losing that opportunity.

00:45:53.020 --> 00:45:56.810
Great leaders probably emerge from that
cauldron.

00:45:56.810 --> 00:46:00.664
How will the new great leaders emerge, I
don't know.

00:46:00.664 --> 00:46:01.817
&gt;&gt; Over here.

00:46:01.817 --> 00:46:04.003
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:46:04.003 --> 00:46:04.830
Thank You.

00:46:04.830 --> 00:46:05.520
Yes.

00:46:05.520 --> 00:46:06.290
&gt;&gt; Andrew Groida.

00:46:06.290 --> 00:46:08.170
I'm a former colleague of Eric and Jared.

00:46:08.170 --> 00:46:08.320
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:46:08.320 --> 00:46:08.410
Hi.

00:46:08.410 --> 00:46:09.570
Nice to see you.

00:46:09.570 --> 00:46:10.490
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:46:10.490 --> 00:46:10.720
Yeah.

00:46:10.720 --> 00:46:13.852
I wanted just to support the point there
is by Candoleeza

00:46:13.852 --> 00:46:18.050
Rice that there is, what you call in your
book confirmation bias.

00:46:18.050 --> 00:46:22.100
That people tend to absorb opinions
similar to their own.

00:46:22.100 --> 00:46:25.754
And we see to large extent that in Russia
those days, which is another

00:46:25.754 --> 00:46:28.402
contradiction over why our population that
still

00:46:28.402 --> 00:46:30.750
believes into what they see on television.

00:46:31.810 --> 00:46:34.130
Yeah, but I have a couple of different
questions.

00:46:34.130 --> 00:46:36.136
You write in your, your books that

00:46:36.136 --> 00:46:40.850
anonymity nowadays can translate to
something like irrelevance.

00:46:40.850 --> 00:46:42.680
Did your opinion evolve since then?

00:46:42.680 --> 00:46:44.762
I remember we had a lot of discussions

00:46:44.762 --> 00:46:47.719
at Google when we started Google Plus
whether people

00:46:47.719 --> 00:46:50.135
should use their real names, and how do
you

00:46:50.135 --> 00:46:53.670
see this in the current developments of
the internet?

00:46:53.670 --> 00:46:56.169
And second how does Google or other

00:46:56.169 --> 00:47:00.265
companies take advantage of the recent
technological progress

00:47:00.265 --> 00:47:04.132
in pictography for example, random number
generators that

00:47:04.132 --> 00:47:07.720
are working in quantum effects and quantum
computers.

00:47:07.720 --> 00:47:10.410
&gt;&gt; So let me answer, answer both of those
quickly.

00:47:10.410 --> 00:47:15.544
There is evidence that people online when
they are anonymous, say things that

00:47:15.544 --> 00:47:20.771
they would never say in front of a human
being or if the name was, was [INAUDIBLE].

00:47:20.771 --> 00:47:24.210
So I think it's important to allow such
speech.

00:47:24.210 --> 00:47:26.060
Certainly you don't want to ban it.

00:47:26.060 --> 00:47:30.906
But you might want to favor identified
speech over unidentified speech.

00:47:30.906 --> 00:47:36.210
Because the consequence of liable and
lying and so forth, you're more likely to

00:47:36.210 --> 00:47:41.369
be truthful if you have a face and a name
and a responsible, roughly speaking.

00:47:41.369 --> 00:47:44.473
So ultimately since Google views
everything kind of as a ranking

00:47:44.473 --> 00:47:46.955
problem I think that's how I would rank
most of it.

00:47:46.955 --> 00:47:49.839
I think it's important to allow anonymous
speech, but

00:47:49.839 --> 00:47:53.399
it's also important to understand that
sometimes anonymous speech

00:47:53.399 --> 00:47:56.036
is not actually true because people would
just sort

00:47:56.036 --> 00:47:58.947
of make stuff up and so there's no
consequence online.

00:47:58.947 --> 00:48:01.470
On the question of cryptography.

00:48:01.470 --> 00:48:04.842
After the NSA revelations, one of the
things that

00:48:04.842 --> 00:48:09.095
the Stone documents showed, and we believe
are true, the

00:48:09.095 --> 00:48:12.869
GCHQ had put a pipe essentially between
our data centers

00:48:12.869 --> 00:48:16.772
and had undone what you know as the Stubby
protocol.

00:48:16.772 --> 00:48:19.961
And we view this as a significant
violation

00:48:19.961 --> 00:48:23.700
inside of the trust and innards of Google.

00:48:23.700 --> 00:48:26.830
So we used very complicated and very
powerful encryption

00:48:26.830 --> 00:48:30.410
now to essentially make it impossible to
do that.

00:48:30.410 --> 00:48:33.930
For the technical people in the room, it's
twenty forty eight bit, twenty

00:48:33.930 --> 00:48:38.210
forty eight bit keys with forward secrecy
we rotate the keys on every transaction.

00:48:38.210 --> 00:48:40.722
Because essentially, we believe,
impossible even

00:48:40.722 --> 00:48:43.324
with modern cryptographic tools to break
that.

00:48:43.324 --> 00:48:46.414
It's possible that the NSA has other
mechanisms

00:48:46.414 --> 00:48:49.730
inside but we certainly don't know of
them.

00:48:49.730 --> 00:48:53.810
And, we've certainly done everything we
can to, to prevent it.

00:48:53.810 --> 00:48:57.010
If the government wants information from
Google, the

00:48:57.010 --> 00:48:59.840
government should follow the law and
follow the

00:48:59.840 --> 00:49:02.500
FISA Court, which was set up under Patriot

00:49:02.500 --> 00:49:05.160
Act One and Patriot Act Two for these
purposes.

00:49:08.310 --> 00:49:09.550
right, I see.

00:49:11.490 --> 00:49:13.290
Oh yes, go ahead, yes.

00:49:13.290 --> 00:49:13.940
&gt;&gt; Hi there.

00:49:13.940 --> 00:49:16.200
My name is Dustin Boyer and I'm a former
Google

00:49:16.200 --> 00:49:19.084
employee, so it's actually nice to see
both of you again.

00:49:19.084 --> 00:49:21.570
I'm sort of fascinated that you guys seem
to be

00:49:21.570 --> 00:49:25.078
so against leaking, because it was the
Snowden documents that

00:49:25.078 --> 00:49:28.076
actually showed that there was this huge
issue with data

00:49:28.076 --> 00:49:31.340
centers, and that so much of Gmail had
been compromised.

00:49:31.340 --> 00:49:34.044
And that the NSA had then, in turn, gone
on

00:49:34.044 --> 00:49:38.119
to use that information to spy and
infiltrate activist groups.

00:49:38.119 --> 00:49:43.207
So, my question to you is, if not Snowden,
then who, because

00:49:43.207 --> 00:49:48.969
obviously the NSA has no incentive to
reveal this type of information.

00:49:48.969 --> 00:49:52.676
In the book we actually talk about this at
some length.

00:49:52.676 --> 00:49:55.124
So the problem here is if I say yes to

00:49:55.124 --> 00:50:00.022
the Snowden thing, what happens when
there's a copycat that isn't

00:50:00.022 --> 00:50:04.413
as judicious about the documents that got
leaked working with

00:50:04.413 --> 00:50:09.830
The Guardian and people start getting
killed and seriously made endanger.

00:50:11.240 --> 00:50:13.110
And it's not at all clear to me that

00:50:13.110 --> 00:50:16.820
you could read the million documents that
you're leaking.

00:50:17.880 --> 00:50:24.300
So to trust any individual with that kind
of empowerment, is of concern.

00:50:24.300 --> 00:50:26.600
So it was certainly helpful to know about
it.

00:50:26.600 --> 00:50:30.785
But it would be much better if we could
have known about

00:50:30.785 --> 00:50:36.762
it without the downside dangers of the
kind, that the leaking implies.

00:50:36.762 --> 00:50:40.368
&gt;&gt; My name's [UNKNOWN] I'm an MBA at the
GSB.

00:50:40.368 --> 00:50:43.198
We spoke extensively about sort of the
bottom

00:50:43.198 --> 00:50:46.251
up adoption of technology, and how that
affects

00:50:46.251 --> 00:50:49.675
revolution and, and democracy, but I was
addressing

00:50:49.675 --> 00:50:53.190
as perspective on sort of more dop down
adoptions.

00:50:53.190 --> 00:50:57.850
Countries like Singapore, which would be
fairly autocratic for a long time.

00:50:57.850 --> 00:50:59.992
But I think an excellent job in using

00:50:59.992 --> 00:51:03.961
technology to empower the citizens, create
better citizen services

00:51:03.961 --> 00:51:06.103
and create a more open society while sort

00:51:06.103 --> 00:51:10.017
of maintaining the benefits of having some
closed-door conversation.

00:51:10.017 --> 00:51:12.685
And all that's often laughed up is being
small, are

00:51:12.685 --> 00:51:16.400
those models, things that people look to
replicate, at other places.

00:51:18.030 --> 00:51:19.619
&gt;&gt; So we spent quite a bit of time with
the prime

00:51:19.619 --> 00:51:22.590
minister of Singapore and he's interviewed
for, is interviewed in the book.

00:51:22.590 --> 00:51:25.660
And tells the story, of how people react
to this social

00:51:25.660 --> 00:51:28.267
information in various examples what
happens

00:51:28.267 --> 00:51:31.700
between the apartment buildings in
Singapore.

00:51:31.700 --> 00:51:33.640
Singapore's done a fantastic job of

00:51:33.640 --> 00:51:37.350
building a knowledge society and connect
everything.

00:51:37.350 --> 00:51:40.434
And they have, for what is largely a one
party

00:51:40.434 --> 00:51:44.750
democracy, they have an unusually open
view of the internet.

00:51:44.750 --> 00:51:48.050
They only block, according to them, 100
sites.

00:51:48.050 --> 00:51:51.780
And they do that in order to point out to
you that they can block it.

00:51:51.780 --> 00:51:55.107
So it may be that culturally because it's
largely Mandarin

00:51:55.107 --> 00:51:58.966
and Chinese, it's a small country,and it'
s of course physically

00:51:58.966 --> 00:52:03.157
smaller, they get a different effect that
wouldn't necessarily scale to

00:52:03.157 --> 00:52:07.850
the hundreds of millions of people of
different religions and interactions.

00:52:07.850 --> 00:52:11.010
And I caution against generalizing from
the, the sort of

00:52:11.010 --> 00:52:14.190
wonderful stories of the Netherlands, and
Singapore and so forth.

00:52:14.190 --> 00:52:18.660
Which are homogeneous, small, ethnic and
largely well run countries.

00:52:18.660 --> 00:52:20.240
&gt;&gt; Eric, do you want to add anything?

00:52:20.240 --> 00:52:23.899
&gt;&gt; Yeah, Eric mentioned is also worth
noting that Singapore is one of only

00:52:23.899 --> 00:52:25.785
two countries in the world whose leader

00:52:25.785 --> 00:52:29.092
is a computer scientist, Estonia is the
other.

00:52:29.092 --> 00:52:33.750
But Eric mentioned a story that the prime
minister shared with us.

00:52:33.750 --> 00:52:36.120
It's actually a story about curry.

00:52:36.120 --> 00:52:38.770
And there, there's a message in the story
that I'll get to in a second.

00:52:38.770 --> 00:52:42.370
Basically, there was a dispute between two
people who shared a hallway, in

00:52:42.370 --> 00:52:46.060
an apartment complex over the smell of
curry in the, in the hallways.

00:52:46.060 --> 00:52:50.235
In typical Singaporean fashion they
brought a mediator in to negotiate

00:52:50.235 --> 00:52:53.956
and everybody was fine until the mediator
went public with her story

00:52:53.956 --> 00:52:58.066
and political opportunists, decided to
seize this as a chance to talk

00:52:58.066 --> 00:53:03.040
about how foreigners were coming in and
taking Singaporean jobs, go figure.

00:53:03.040 --> 00:53:07.340
A bunch of people got together online and
declared a national day of curry cooking.

00:53:07.340 --> 00:53:10.020
And the prime, and the prime minister, to

00:53:10.020 --> 00:53:13.730
his credit, acknowledged that this really
scared them.

00:53:13.730 --> 00:53:15.160
And he said that they started to see

00:53:15.160 --> 00:53:18.320
people, that he started to see descent
build online.

00:53:18.320 --> 00:53:20.130
And he acknowledged that they overreacted
to it

00:53:20.130 --> 00:53:23.080
and the overreaction to what was in fact a

00:53:23.080 --> 00:53:25.430
very small number of people really having
good fun

00:53:25.430 --> 00:53:28.140
with something, ended up putting people in
the streets.

00:53:28.140 --> 00:53:33.520
And the lesson from this is there's a
dilemma that autocratic leaders will face

00:53:33.520 --> 00:53:34.850
in the future which is having to

00:53:34.850 --> 00:53:38.310
distinguish between what's real and what's
noise.

00:53:38.310 --> 00:53:41.330
And many autocrats in the future, at some
point, they'll overreact

00:53:41.330 --> 00:53:45.280
to that noise and create a disturbance
that ultimately they can't control.

00:53:49.120 --> 00:53:50.070
&gt;&gt; I'm Theo Milonopoulos.

00:53:50.070 --> 00:53:53.700
I'm a Stanford alum and PhD student in
political science.

00:53:53.700 --> 00:53:59.817
Doctor Schmidt I wanted reinforce a point
that Doctor Rice made about the

00:53:59.817 --> 00:54:03.128
motivations that the NSA and companies

00:54:03.128 --> 00:54:07.476
