00:54:07.476 --> 00:54:10.541
It seems to me that the NSA, if it could
get out

00:54:10.541 --> 00:54:15.680
from the business of data collection it
would do it in a heartbeat.

00:54:15.680 --> 00:54:19.840
In the sense that the, the larger and

00:54:19.840 --> 00:54:23.520
larger amounts of information that it
collects makes it's

00:54:23.520 --> 00:54:26.330
job actually more difficult in the sense
that it

00:54:26.330 --> 00:54:29.890
becomes finding a needle in a much larger
haystack.

00:54:29.890 --> 00:54:33.832
So, it, it seems to me that, that the, the
NSA would love

00:54:33.832 --> 00:54:39.840
not to have to collect this data because,
but the terrorist threats keep coming.

00:54:39.840 --> 00:54:43.730
And that the day on September 12th, the
question won't

00:54:43.730 --> 00:54:48.366
be, why didn't the NSA do more to protect
my privacy.

00:54:48.366 --> 00:54:51.353
It will be why didn't it find the needle
in the haystack?

00:54:51.353 --> 00:54:52.619
&gt;&gt; My second point

00:54:52.619 --> 00:54:54.300
&gt;&gt; Can I actually, can I actually respond
to that?

00:54:54.300 --> 00:54:54.891
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:54:54.891 --> 00:54:57.732
&gt;&gt; In the book we actually make your
argument in the following context.

00:54:57.732 --> 00:55:03.082
We say that in the fight between hawks,
and doves the hawks always win.

00:55:03.082 --> 00:55:05.836
Because the downside risk of a small
terrorist

00:55:05.836 --> 00:55:08.448
act, and the, and the if you will, the

00:55:08.448 --> 00:55:10.989
loss of diversity and fear and so forth
that

00:55:10.989 --> 00:55:15.960
comes with everyone being slightly worried
about being surveillance.

00:55:15.960 --> 00:55:18.790
There, there's no, the doves don't have a,
a standing.

00:55:18.790 --> 00:55:21.550
It ultimately is an asymmetric outcome.

00:55:21.550 --> 00:55:23.300
I disagree a little bit with the way you

00:55:23.300 --> 00:55:26.950
phrased that, just as a matter of computer
science.

00:55:26.950 --> 00:55:30.030
Modern algorithms are capable of doing

00:55:30.030 --> 00:55:33.720
extraordinarily subtle things with large
data collections.

00:55:33.720 --> 00:55:36.603
So, for example, the metadata that
consists of your the

00:55:36.603 --> 00:55:39.665
phone numbers you call, is highly, highly
interesting to a

00:55:39.665 --> 00:55:42.547
whole bunch of computer scientists who
want to figure out

00:55:42.547 --> 00:55:46.308
who you are, who you're associated with,
and so forth.

00:55:46.308 --> 00:55:49.460
So it's highly valuable to the NSA which
is why they did it.

00:55:49.460 --> 00:55:51.806
I'm on the task force for the White House
trying

00:55:51.806 --> 00:55:53.742
to figure out what to do with this data by

00:55:53.742 --> 00:55:56.088
the way, and the President has asked that
it not

00:55:56.088 --> 00:55:58.990
be stored by the NSA but be stored
somewhere else.

00:55:58.990 --> 00:56:01.612
Well, there is not agreement on where that
information

00:56:01.612 --> 00:56:04.290
should be stored for the reasons that I
outlined.

00:56:04.290 --> 00:56:04.570
Go ahead.

00:56:04.570 --> 00:56:04.750
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:56:04.750 --> 00:56:08.974
&gt;&gt; And, and, and, and just because we
talked, touched on the, the question

00:56:08.974 --> 00:56:13.590
about how to get to a more elevated
political di, discussion on these issues.

00:56:13.590 --> 00:56:16.410
I don't believe like, the labels of hawks
